From a90f4ff3a2d4617068751b397f41e423f68b1441 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 01:02:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Fix Purchase Navigator reports getting permanently stuck
 at "processing" after real payment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Real incident: a paid buying submission got stuck at status:'processing'
forever, spinning on navigator-status.html with no resolution. Root cause:
this Vercel project is on the Hobby plan, which hard-caps a serverless
function invocation at 60 seconds (see vercel.json's maxDuration — not
something raisable without a plan upgrade). generatePurchaseReport was
doing up to 2 full attempts (each potentially a web_search round plus a
forced follow-up call) inside ONE invocation, which can exceed 60s. When
Vercel kills a function past its limit, it does so outside this codebase's
own try/catch, so the row was left exactly as it was at the moment of the
kill — 'processing', no error, no way to retry, since the trigger in
get-navigator-submission.js only re-ran generation for status:'paid'.

Fixes, bundled together since they're all part of the same incident:

1. generatePurchaseReport now makes exactly ONE attempt per invocation,
   tracked via a new generation_attempts column. A recoverable failure
   (incomplete report, contamination, or a normal API error) hands status
   back to 'paid' so the next client poll (already happening every 3s)
   triggers a fresh attempt with its own full 60s budget, instead of
   stacking attempts inside a single request. Once MAX_ATTEMPTS (2) is
   truly exhausted, it fails properly with a real error message.

2. get-navigator-submission.js now also treats a buying submission stuck at
   'processing' for more than 70 seconds (safely past the 60s ceiling) as
   eligible for another attempt — recovering a submission that got hard-
   killed mid-flight, rather than leaving it stuck forever.

3. web_search max_uses trimmed from 5 to 3 to reduce a single attempt's
   worst-case latency against the same 60s ceiling.

4. reportLooksContaminated (from the previous diagnostics patch) now also
   surfaces which substrings it matched in the stored error, so a future
   contamination rejection is debuggable without re-running anything.

5. New/updated tests: single-attempt-per-call behavior verified as two
   separate generatePurchaseReport calls (matching how two separate polls
   actually behave), exhausted-attempts fails fast without another API
   call, and a new test file covers the stuck-processing recovery path in
   get-navigator-submission.js specifically (5 tests: recovers when
   genuinely stuck, does not race a fresh in-flight attempt, 'paid' path
   unaffected, other products unaffected, thrown errors don't 500 the
   endpoint). 31 tests total, all passing.

Also requires a small Supabase migration (already applied directly, not
part of this patch): navigator_submissions gets a new generation_attempts
integer column, default 0.

The underlying constraint — 60s hard cap — is a Hobby-plan limit. This
patch makes the product reliable within that limit, but upgrading to
Vercel Pro (which allows up to 300s per function) would remove the need
for this kind of split-attempt workaround entirely and is worth considering
if generation attempts still time out in practice after this ships.
